WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.250
One carpenter can build a shed.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.120
A house needs an electrician,

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:08.720
a plumber,

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:09.520
and a roofer,

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:10.870
working in parallel,

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.470
each doing what they do best.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.320
Agents work the same way.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.690
But parallel agents need coordination,

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.550
and coordination has a cost.

00:00:19.275 --> 00:00:21.325
The hard part isn't running several agents.

00:00:21.395 --> 00:00:22.615
It's splitting the work.

00:00:22.615 --> 00:00:25.005
If one agent depends on another's output,

00:00:25.015 --> 00:00:26.345
they can't run in parallel.

00:00:26.695 --> 00:00:28.175
So find the seams.

00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:29.415
Split by layer,

00:00:29.415 --> 00:00:31.405
with a clear contract between them.

00:00:31.555 --> 00:00:32.955
Split by feature.

00:00:32.955 --> 00:00:34.585
Or split by concern:

00:00:34.855 --> 00:00:35.975
one refactors,

00:00:35.975 --> 00:00:37.285
one writes tests,

00:00:37.295 --> 00:00:38.725
one updates the docs.

00:00:39.509 --> 00:00:41.529
Every agent gets its own branch and

00:00:41.529 --> 00:00:42.579
its own work tree.

00:00:42.589 --> 00:00:44.949
Two agents in one working directory will

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:46.489
trample each other's files.

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:47.909
Three directories,

00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:48.879
three branches,

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.019
three agents.

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:53.179
Multi -agent work comes in two shapes.

00:00:53.409 --> 00:00:55.749
Sub -agents inside one session buy you

00:00:55.749 --> 00:00:56.959
clean context.

00:00:57.029 --> 00:00:59.769
One reads 40 files and hands back

00:00:59.769 --> 00:01:01.099
a 10 -line summary.

00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:02.789
Background agents take a whole,

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:05.109
well -scoped ticket and come back later

00:01:05.109 --> 00:01:06.229
with a pull request.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:07.409
For a vague task,

00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:08.259
use neither.

00:01:08.993 --> 00:01:11.143
Sometimes agents work in sequence.

00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:12.123
One plans,

00:01:12.173 --> 00:01:13.253
one implements,

00:01:13.253 --> 00:01:14.183
one reviews.

00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:16.163
The danger is the handover.

00:01:16.173 --> 00:01:18.133
The next agent starts cold.

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:19.483
So make it explicit:

00:01:19.573 --> 00:01:20.843
a plan file,

00:01:20.853 --> 00:01:21.983
stub functions,

00:01:22.073 --> 00:01:23.523
a progress checklist.

00:01:23.713 --> 00:01:24.893
Write it like a ticket for a

00:01:24.893 --> 00:01:26.383
colleague on another continent.

00:01:27.211 --> 00:01:28.541
Then comes the merge.

00:01:28.551 --> 00:01:30.341
One agent changed the schema,

00:01:30.351 --> 00:01:32.171
another wrote a migration for the old

00:01:32.171 --> 00:01:32.731
one.

00:01:32.731 --> 00:01:35.051
Prevent conflicts by giving each agent its

00:01:35.051 --> 00:01:35.931
own files.

00:01:35.931 --> 00:01:37.811
Let a merge agent handle the rest.

00:01:37.811 --> 00:01:39.351
And review everything yourself,

00:01:39.351 --> 00:01:41.851
because code can merge cleanly and still

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:43.001
contradict itself.

00:01:43.831 --> 00:01:45.701
More agents isn't always better.

00:01:45.751 --> 00:01:47.661
With four terminals open,

00:01:47.691 --> 00:01:49.631
one agent can crash silently while you

00:01:49.631 --> 00:01:50.271
look elsewhere.

00:01:50.271 --> 00:01:52.071
You become the bottleneck.

00:01:52.071 --> 00:01:54.591
And every extra agent adds setup,

00:01:54.591 --> 00:01:55.211
merging,

00:01:55.211 --> 00:01:55.911
and review.

00:01:55.911 --> 00:01:57.131
For short tasks,

00:01:57.131 --> 00:01:58.781
the overhead eats the gains.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:00.719
Here's where it pays.

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:02.459
Picture a notifications feature:

00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:04.009
a backend endpoint,

00:02:04.019 --> 00:02:05.059
a bell icon,

00:02:05.059 --> 00:02:06.069
and the tests.

00:02:06.179 --> 00:02:07.999
Write the contract first.

00:02:07.999 --> 00:02:09.719
Then three agents run at once,

00:02:09.719 --> 00:02:11.579
and you merge the backend first.

00:02:11.579 --> 00:02:13.559
About forty-five minutes in total,

00:02:13.559 --> 00:02:15.889
against roughly ninety for one agent alone.

00:02:16.743 --> 00:02:18.483
A crew of five isn't always faster

00:02:18.483 --> 00:02:20.493
than one sailor who knows the boat.

00:02:20.563 --> 00:02:22.543
The skill isn't running as many agents

00:02:22.543 --> 00:02:23.463
as possible,

00:02:23.463 --> 00:02:25.343
it's knowing which pieces are safe to

00:02:25.343 --> 00:02:26.353
run in parallel.

00:02:26.443 --> 00:02:28.783
That judgment is the highest leverage prompt

00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:29.773
you can write.

00:02:29.783 --> 00:02:30.663
Next up,

00:02:30.663 --> 00:02:31.973
Agents in the Pipeline.
